<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>MemoryManagement - Tag - loommii</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/memorymanagement/</link><description>我的个人技术博客</description><generator>Hugo 0.165.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 16 Jan 2026 01:28:43 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/memorymanagement/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Go Daily Question 190</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_190/</link><pubDate>Wed, 19 Feb 2025 02:08:43 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_190/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What are the scenarios for memory leaks?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Long-lived objects holding references to short-lived objects: For example, objects referenced by global variables.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Unclosed Goroutines: If a Goroutine starts but fails to terminate normally due to some reason (e.g., deadlock, infinite loop, channel blocking), it will continue to occupy memory.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Improper use of the &lt;code&gt;time&lt;/code&gt; package.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Improper string operations.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Unclosed resources like files and network connections.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Improper CGO memory management: If your Go code uses CGO and allocates memory in C code, you need to manually free that memory. Otherwise, it will lead to a memory leak.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go: What Happens with Uninitialized Slice and Map Operations?</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/what_happens_if_go_map_slice_is_not_initialized/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Dec 2024 19:03:04 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/what_happens_if_go_map_slice_is_not_initialized/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/techarticle/">TechArticle</category><description>Detailed explanation of the behavior and impact of uninitialized slice and map operations in Go.</description></item><item><title>Go: Slices vs. Arrays</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/difference_between_go_slice_and_array/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Dec 2024 16:10:18 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/difference_between_go_slice_and_array/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/techarticle/">TechArticle</category><description>A detailed explanation of the differences between slices and arrays in Go.</description></item><item><title>An Overview of Go GC (Garbage Collection)</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/a_brief_discussion_on_go_gc_garbage_collection/</link><pubDate>Mon, 09 Dec 2024 13:03:08 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/a_brief_discussion_on_go_gc_garbage_collection/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/techarticle/">TechArticle</category><description>Detailed explanation of the principles and implementation of Go's garbage collection mechanism.</description></item><item><title>The Relationship Between Programs and Memory</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/the_relationship_between_program_and_memory/</link><pubDate>Sun, 08 Dec 2024 00:28:20 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/posts/the_relationship_between_program_and_memory/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/techarticle/">TechArticle</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="the-relationship-between-programs-and-memory"&gt;&lt;span&gt